(Regression: `<buffer> as text` yields an independent copy of the
buffer's bytes — bug #41. The cast used to return a pointer into the
buffer's data area, which was wrong twice over. Rewriting the buffer
silently rewrote every text taken from it — the aliasing half, a
correctness bug on its own with no diagnostic. And resizing the buffer
frees the old allocation, as LANGUAGE.md's "Buffer Resizing" says it
does, so reading such a text afterwards was a use-after-free: both
resize directions segfaulted. Copying at the cast fixes both halves.

Each case below reads the text AFTER the buffer it came from has been
rewritten, shrunk, or grown. On the unfixed compiler the aliasing case
printed `SECOND` and every resize case crashed.)

(The aliasing half: clearing and refilling the buffer must not touch a
text already taken from it, and the buffer itself must still change.)
a buffer called 'the line buffer' is 64 bytes in size.
append "first" to 'the line buffer'.
a text called 'the first line' is 'the line buffer' as text.
Print 'the first line'.
clear 'the line buffer'.
append "SECOND" to 'the line buffer'.
Print 'the first line'.
Print 'the line buffer' as text.

(Shrinking the source frees its old allocation; the copy is untouched.)
a buffer called 'the sentence buffer' is 512 bytes in size.
append "the quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog" to 'the sentence buffer'.
a text called 'the saved sentence' is 'the sentence buffer' as text.
resize 'the sentence buffer' to 4 bytes.
Print 'the saved sentence'.

(Growing it reallocates just the same, so the copy must survive that too.)
a buffer called 'the greeting buffer' is 16 bytes in size.
append "hello" to 'the greeting buffer'.
a text called 'the saved greeting' is 'the greeting buffer' as text.
resize 'the greeting buffer' to 4096 bytes.
Print 'the saved greeting'.

(Two texts from one buffer are two separate copies, not two aliases.)
a buffer called 'the shared buffer' is 128 bytes in size.
append "alpha" to 'the shared buffer'.
a text called 'the first copy' is 'the shared buffer' as text.
a text called 'the second copy' is 'the shared buffer' as text.
resize 'the shared buffer' to 8 bytes.
Print 'the first copy'.
Print 'the second copy'.

(A copy longer than the initial capacity of a fresh buffer has to grow
during the copy, which moves the destination — the cast must return the
pointer it got back, not the one it started with.)
a buffer called 'the long buffer' is 8192 bytes in size.
a number called 'chunks written' is 0.
While 'chunks written' is less than 500,
    append "0123456789" to 'the long buffer',
    Set 'chunks written' to 'chunks written' add 1.
a text called 'the long copy' is 'the long buffer' as text.
resize 'the long buffer' to 4 bytes.
a buffer called 'the length probe' is 8192 bytes in size.
append 'the long copy' to 'the length probe'.
Print 'the length probe's length.

(An empty buffer converts to empty text, not to a stray byte.)
a buffer called 'the empty buffer' is 16 bytes in size.
a text called 'the empty text' is 'the empty buffer' as text.
Print "empty text follows".
Print 'the empty text'.
Print "empty text above".
